<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sudetenland</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sudetenland was the name used in the 20th century for the border regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-silesia">Czech Silesia</a> inhabited mainly by German speakers. Claimed by German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1918, it was ceded to Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich-agreement">Munich Agreement</a> of 1938. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> most of its roughly three million Germans were expelled to Germany and Austria.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sudetenland.jpg" alt="Sudetenland" width="320" height="188" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fext · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sudetendeutsche.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The areas later called the Sudetenland never formed a single historical region, and their history is hard to separate from that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> before nationalism arrived in the 19th century. German settlers were invited into the Bohemian border regions from the second half of the 13th century, especially by the Přemyslid kings Ottokar II and Wenceslaus II, and German speakers remained dominant there through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussite-wars">Hussite Wars</a>. The word Sudeten comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudetes">Sudeten Mountains</a>. The publicist Franz Jesser in 1902 first applied Sudetenland to all the areas of Bohemia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> and Austrian Silesia with a German-speaking majority, and the term became common in the 1920s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> broke apart in October 1918, German deputies from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> and Silesia citing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson">Woodrow Wilson</a>'s Fourteen Points tried to unite their regions with the Republic of German Austria, and four provinces were proclaimed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechs">Czechs</a> rejected this and demanded the inclusion of the lands inhabited by Germans, which they said had always been part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lands-of-the-bohemian-crown">Bohemian Crown</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-saint-germain-en-laye-1919">Treaty of Saint-Germain</a> in 1919 confirmed their place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>. The 1921 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 3,123,000 German speakers, 23.4% of the population. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a> hit the region's export industries hard, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glassmaking">glassmaking</a> and textiles, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudeten-german-party">Sudeten German Party</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-henlein">Konrad Henlein</a> gained great popularity.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anschluss">Anschluss</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in March 1938, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> made himself the advocate of the Germans in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>. Lord Runciman's mediation failed, and on 15 September Neville Chamberlain agreed with Hitler to the cession, as did Édouard Daladier three days later; no Czechoslovak representative was invited. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> on 29 September Hitler, Daladier and Chamberlain, on the proposal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a>, signed the agreement that allowed the immediate occupation of the Sudetenland, which took place between 1 and 10 October 1938. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> annexed part of the borderland in 1938. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> invaded the rest of the Czech lands in March 1939. The Sudetenland was divided, and its northern and western parts became the Reichsgau Sudetenland, governed by Henlein.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After the war the Potsdam Conference decided that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudeten-germans">Sudeten Germans</a> would have to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, and the overwhelming majority were expelled, about 500,000 of them in the spring and summer of 1945. In 1950 only 159,938 Germans were left of 3,149,820 in 1930. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechs">Czechs</a> mostly, and also Slovaks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and others, resettled the region. The Sudeten German dialects have largely disappeared.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sudetenland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At Munich the great powers decided its fate without it, and the army surrendered the fortified <b>Sudetenland</b> without a fight.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>When Germany remilitarised the Rhineland on 7 March 1936, nothing was done, and in 1938 Édouard Daladier signed the Munich Agreement, which gave the <b>Sudetenland</b> to Nazi Germany.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-third-republic">French Third Republic · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After annexing Austria and the <b>Sudetenland</b> in 1938 and occupying Prague in March 1939, Germany invaded Poland on 1 September 1939, and Britain and France declared war.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Chamberlain, Daladier, Hitler and Mussolini agreed at Munich that Czechoslovakia must cede the <b>Sudetenland</b>; the deal was signed after midnight.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-29">29 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Munich Agreement and the loss of the <b>Sudetenland</b> in 1938, Germany occupied the rest of Bohemia and Moravia on 15 March 1939, as Slovakia declared independence.</blockquote><a href="/state/protectorate-of-bohemia-and-moravia">Protectorate of Bohemia and Moravia</a></li><li><blockquote>The republic claimed the <b>Sudetenland</b>, South Tyrol and other German-speaking areas, but could not enforce these claims.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-german-austria">Republic of German-Austria</a></li></ul></section>
